Interview Questions & Structured Answers

Q1. Explain the problem in your own words.
We need to determine whether every character required by the ransom note is available in the magazine.
Each character in the magazine can only be used once.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q2. What is the brute-force solution?
For each character in the ransom note:

Search the magazine.
If found, remove or mark that character.
If not found, return false.

Time Complexity: O(n × m)
----------------------------------

Q3. Why is brute force inefficient?
Because we repeatedly search the magazine for every character in the ransom note. 
This causes many repeated scans, making the solution much slower for larger strings.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Why use a frequency array instead of searching?
A frequency array stores how many times each character appears.
Instead of searching repeatedly, we can check and update the count in constant time.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q5. Why is the array size 26?
The problem guarantees that all characters are lowercase English letters (a to z), so we need one position for each of the 26 letters.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q6. Explain freq[c - 'a'].
Characters are converted into array indexes.

'a' - 'a' = 0
'b' - 'a' = 1
'c' - 'a' = 2
...
'z' - 'a' = 25

This allows direct access to each character's count.
---------------------------------------------------

Q7. Why do we increment the counts first?
We first record how many letters are available in the magazine.
Only after knowing the available supply can we start consuming letters for the ransom note.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q8. Why decrement while reading the ransom note?
Each character in the ransom note uses one occurrence from the magazine.
Decrementing simulates consuming that character.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q9. Why check for freq[c - 'a'] < 0?
A negative count means we've tried to use a character more times than it exists in the magazine, so constructing the ransom note is impossible.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q10. Why is the space complexity O(1)?
The frequency array always has exactly 26 elements, regardless of the input size. 
Since its size never changes, the extra space used is constant.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q11. Could we use an unordered_map instead?
Yes. An unordered_map<char, int> works and is useful if the character set is large or unknown (for example, Unicode).
However, since this problem only uses lowercase English letters, a fixed-size array is simpler and faster.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Explanation (2 Minutes)

"The problem asks whether the ransom note can be constructed using the letters in the magazine, where each letter can only be used once.
Instead of repeatedly searching the magazine, I count the frequency of each letter using an array of size 26 because only lowercase English letters are allowed.
Then I iterate through the ransom note and decrement the corresponding count for each character. 
If any count becomes negative, it means we've used that character more times than it exists in the magazine, so I return false. 
If I finish processing all characters without any negative count, I return true. This solution runs in O(m + n) time and uses O(1) extra space.`
